
# Task 04 – Savings Account Mini-Statement

## Aim
To create a SavingsAccount class using an array to store the last 10 transactions.

## 1. Class Specification
The SavingsAccount class contains private members:
- accountNumber
- balance
- transactions[10]
- count

Public member functions:
- deposit()
- withdraw()
- miniStatement()
- displayBalance()

## 2. Access Specifiers
Private members cannot be accessed directly outside the class.

Public functions provide controlled access to the account information.

This protects the account data from unwanted modification.

## 3. Member Functions
The deposit() function increases the account balance.

The withdraw() function decreases the balance if sufficient funds are available.

The miniStatement() function displays the stored transactions.

Some functions are defined inside the class, while others are defined outside using the scope resolution operator (::).

## 4. Handling the 11th Transaction
When the transaction array is full, the oldest transaction is removed.

The remaining transactions are shifted one position to the left.

The new transaction is stored at the last position.

## 5. Alternative Concept
A circular buffer can be used instead of shifting the array.

A circular buffer maintains a starting index and replaces the oldest transaction when full.

This avoids shifting all elements.

A std::deque can also use push_back() and pop_front().

## 6. Complexity Analysis

Fixed array with shifting:
Insertion when full: O(k), where k is the number of stored transactions.

Circular buffer:
Insertion: O(1).

Deque:
Insertion and removal at opposite ends: O(1) amortized for push_back and O(1) for pop_front.

Space complexity:
O(10) for the fixed transaction array.
O(10) for a bounded circular buffer or deque holding ten transactions.

## 7. Design Decision
For storing the last 50 transactions, a circular buffer is a suitable design because it avoids shifting the stored transactions each time a new transaction arrives.

## Result
The SavingsAccount class successfully demonstrates encapsulation, arrays inside a class, member functions and transaction management.
